(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-02"] = {"phy-12-02-m01":{"idea":"Pressure under a liquid is the surface pressure plus the weight of the column above.","steps":["Each metre of liquid adds the weight of a column: ρ g h.","The surface is open to the atmosphere at P₀.","So the total pressure is P = P₀ + ρ g h."],"others":"ρgh alone is the gauge pressure, which leaves out the atmosphere. P₀ − ρgh would make pressure fall with depth. P₀ + ρh/g has the wrong units.","trap":"Total pressure includes the atmosphere. Gauge pressure does not.","answer":"P₀ + ρgh"},"phy-12-02-m02":{"idea":"Pascal's law says that pressure applied to a confined fluid spreads everywhere.","steps":["Press on an enclosed fluid at rest.","The extra pressure is passed on to every part of the fluid.","It also reaches the container walls, without any loss."],"others":"It is not felt only at the walls, not weaker with distance, and not only along the applied force.","trap":"Pressure goes in all directions equally.","answer":"transmitted undiminished to every part of the fluid and to the walls"},"phy-12-02-m03":{"idea":"Archimedes' principle: the upthrust equals the weight of the fluid pushed aside.","steps":["A body in a fluid pushes some fluid out of the way.","The fluid pushes back with a force equal to the weight of that displaced fluid.","That force is the buoyant force."],"others":"It is not the weight of the body, the density of the fluid, or the weight of all the fluid in the container.","trap":"Displaced fluid, not total fluid.","answer":"the weight of the fluid it displaces"},"phy-12-02-m04":{"idea":"A body floats when the upthrust balances its weight.","steps":["At equilibrium: buoyant force = weight of the body.","The buoyant force is the weight of the displaced liquid.","So the displaced liquid weighs the same as the body."],"others":"Being hollow is not a requirement. A density greater than the liquid would make it sink. An upthrust greater than the weight would make it rise.","trap":"A floating body displaces its own weight of liquid.","answer":"the weight of the liquid it displaces equals its own weight"},"phy-12-02-m05":{"idea":"For a steady incompressible flow, the same volume passes every cross-section each second.","steps":["Volume per second = area × speed.","This must be equal at both sections.","So A₁ v₁ = A₂ v₂ (the equation of continuity)."],"others":"A₁v₂ = A₂v₁ swaps the speeds. A₁/v₁ = A₂/v₂ and A₁ + v₁ = A₂ + v₂ do not express volume flow.","trap":"Narrow pipe means faster flow.","answer":"A₁v₁ = A₂v₂"},"phy-12-02-m06":{"idea":"Bernoulli's equation is energy conservation for a moving fluid.","steps":["It adds pressure energy, kinetic energy and potential energy, all per unit volume.","The kinetic part is ½ρv² and the potential part is ρgh.","So P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant."],"others":"P + ρv + ρgh has the wrong kinetic term. The other two have wrong signs.","trap":"All three terms are added, and the kinetic term has the ½ and the square.","answer":"P + ½ρv² + ρgh is constant"},"phy-12-02-m07":{"idea":"A fluid speeds up in a narrow section and its pressure drops.","steps":["Continuity: a narrower pipe gives a higher speed.","The pipe is horizontal, so h does not change.","Bernoulli: higher ½ρv² means lower P."],"others":"Lower speed and higher pressure is the wide section. Both unchanged ignores continuity. Higher speed and higher pressure contradicts Bernoulli.","trap":"Fast flow, low pressure.","answer":"the speed is higher and the pressure is lower"},"phy-12-02-m08":{"idea":"The unit of viscosity comes from the formula F = ηA(dv/dx).","steps":["η = F dx/(A dv).","Units: N × m/(m² × m/s) = N s/m².","N/m² is the pascal, so the unit is Pa s."],"others":"N/m is surface tension. Pa/s and N s/s do not match the formula.","trap":"Viscosity: pascal second.","answer":"pascal second (Pa s)"},"phy-12-02-m09":{"idea":"Stokes' law gives the drag on a small sphere moving slowly through a fluid.","steps":["The drag force depends on η, r and v.","For slow smooth flow: F = 6π η r v.","It is proportional to the radius, not its square."],"others":"4πηrv has the wrong number. 6πηr²v has r squared. πηrv² uses v squared, which is for fast flow.","trap":"6π η r v.","answer":"6πηrv"},"phy-12-02-m10":{"idea":"Terminal speed goes as the square of the radius.","steps":["v_t = 2r²(ρ − σ)g/(9η).","So v_t ∝ r².","Doubling r gives 2² = 4 times the speed."],"others":"8 : 1 would need r³. 2 : 1 would need r. 1 : 2 is upside down.","trap":"Radius squared, not cubed.","answer":"4 : 1"},"phy-12-02-m11":{"idea":"Terminal velocity is reached when the forces balance and the acceleration is zero.","steps":["Weight pulls down. Upthrust and drag push up.","Drag grows as the speed grows.","At terminal velocity: weight = upthrust + drag, so the net force is zero."],"others":"The upthrust is never greater than the weight in a falling body. The speed is not zero. The drag is not zero.","trap":"Zero net force means constant speed, not zero speed.","answer":"weight = upthrust + viscous drag, so the net force is zero"},"phy-12-02-m12":{"idea":"Surface tension is force per unit length.","steps":["T = F/L.","Unit: newton per metre (N/m).","It also equals energy per unit area: J/m²."],"others":"J/s is power. N/m² is pressure. N m is torque or energy.","trap":"N/m for tension, N/m² for pressure.","answer":"newton per metre (N/m)"},"phy-12-02-m13":{"idea":"A soap bubble has two surfaces, so its excess pressure is double that of a drop.","steps":["A drop has one surface: ΔP = 2T/R.","A soap film has an inner and an outer surface.","So ΔP = 2 × 2T/R = 4T/R."],"others":"T/R and 8T/R are wrong factors. 2T/R is for a drop.","trap":"Bubble: 4T/R. Drop: 2T/R.","answer":"4T/R"},"phy-12-02-m14":{"idea":"A liquid drop has a single free surface.","steps":["With one surface, the pressure difference is ΔP = 2T/R.","This comes from balancing surface tension against pressure on a half drop."],"others":"4T/R is for a soap bubble. 2TR and T/(2R) are not correct forms.","trap":"Drop: 2T/R.","answer":"2T/R"},"phy-12-02-m15":{"idea":"The angle of contact decides whether a liquid rises or falls in a thin tube.","steps":["For water on glass the angle of contact is acute, so the meniscus curves up and the water rises.","For mercury on glass the angle is obtuse, so the meniscus curves down and mercury is pushed down.","The difference is in the angle of contact."],"others":"Viscosity, density and surface tension alone do not decide the sign of the effect.","trap":"Acute: rises. Obtuse: falls.","answer":"the angle of contact is acute for water and obtuse for mercury"},"phy-12-02-m16":{"idea":"Capillary rise is inversely proportional to the tube radius.","steps":["h = 2T cos θ/(ρ g r).","So h ∝ 1/r.","Twice the radius gives half the rise."],"others":"The same rise or a larger rise would need h to grow or stay with r. Four times is the wrong direction.","trap":"Thin tubes lift liquid higher.","answer":"half as much"},"phy-12-02-m17":{"idea":"The Reynolds number tells if flow is smooth or turbulent.","steps":["Re = ρ v D/η.","It combines the inertia effects (density, speed, diameter) and viscosity.","Below about 2000 in a pipe the flow is laminar. Above about 4000 it is turbulent."],"others":"The other three put the quantities in the wrong places, so they would not be dimensionless.","trap":"Density, speed and diameter on top. Viscosity below.","answer":"ρvD/η"},"phy-12-02-m18":{"idea":"Heating changes viscosity in opposite ways in liquids and gases.","steps":["In a liquid, warm molecules slip past each other more easily, so viscosity falls.","In a gas, faster molecules carry more momentum between layers, so viscosity rises.","So the liquid's decreases and the gas's increases."],"others":"The other options reverse the trends or treat both the same.","trap":"Honey flows faster when warm; hot air is more viscous than cold air.","answer":"the liquid's decreases and the gas's increases"},"phy-12-02-m19":{"idea":"Torricelli's theorem: efflux speed equals the speed of a body falling through the same depth.","steps":["Apply Bernoulli between the free surface (speed nearly zero) and the hole.","P₀ + ρgh = P₀ + ½ρv².","So v = √(2gh)."],"others":"gh has the wrong units. √(gh) misses the factor 2. 2√(gh) has the wrong factor.","trap":"Same as v = √(2gh) for a free fall through h.","answer":"√(2gh)"},"phy-12-02-m20":{"idea":"A hydraulic lift multiplies force by the ratio of the areas.","steps":["Pressure is the same in both pistons: F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂.","So F₂ = F₁ A₂/A₁.","A big A₂ gives a big force."],"others":"F₁(A₂ − A₁) uses a difference. F₁ alone would mean no gain. F₁A₁/A₂ gives a smaller force.","trap":"Force is multiplied, but the small piston must move farther.","answer":"F₁A₂/A₁"},"phy-12-02-m21":{"idea":"A wing lifts because air moves faster over the top, where the pressure is lower.","steps":["The shape of the wing makes air above it move faster.","Bernoulli: faster air means lower pressure.","Pressure below is higher, so the net push is upwards."],"others":"Faster air at a higher pressure contradicts Bernoulli. Equal pressure gives no lift. Slower air with lower pressure also contradicts Bernoulli.","trap":"Lift comes from a pressure difference.","answer":"the air above is faster and at lower pressure than the air below"},"phy-12-02-m22":{"idea":"For a floating body, the submerged fraction equals the density ratio.","steps":["Weight = upthrust: ρ_ice V g = ρ_water V_sub g.","V_sub/V = ρ_ice/ρ_water = 900/1000.","That is 0.9, or 90%."],"others":"81% squares the ratio. 10% is the part above water. 100% would mean it sinks completely.","trap":"10% of an iceberg is above water for density ratio 0.9.","answer":"90%"},"phy-12-02-m23":{"idea":"Surface tension is also energy per unit area, so extra surface needs work.","steps":["T = energy per unit area.","Increasing the area by ΔA stores T ΔA of energy.","So the work done is W = T ΔA."],"others":"ΔA/T, T/ΔA and TΔA² have the wrong units.","trap":"Work = T × extra area.","answer":"TΔA"},"phy-12-02-m24":{"idea":"Excess pressure in a bubble is inversely proportional to its radius.","steps":["ΔP = 4T/R.","T is the same and R becomes 2R.","So ΔP becomes half."],"others":"It is not unchanged, and not double or four times, because R is in the denominator.","trap":"Bigger bubbles have less excess pressure.","answer":"becomes half"},"phy-12-02-m25":{"idea":"When drops merge, the volume stays the same.","steps":["Volume of 8 small drops = 8 × (4/3)π r³.","Volume of the big drop = (4/3)π R³.","Equal volumes: R³ = 8 r³, so R = 2r."],"others":"r√2, 8r and 4r come from wrong power laws (area or direct proportion).","trap":"Conserve volume, not area.","answer":"2r"},"phy-12-02-m26":{"idea":"Immersed bodies feel lighter because of the buoyant force.","steps":["Apparent weight = true weight − upthrust.","The upthrust is the weight of the displaced liquid.","So it weighs less than its true weight by that amount."],"others":"It is not equal to the true weight, since there is upthrust. It is not more than the true weight. It is not just the buoyant force.","trap":"A fully immersed body does not lose its mass, only its apparent weight.","answer":"less than its true weight by the buoyant force"},"phy-12-02-m27":{"idea":"Pressure has no direction of its own, so it is a scalar.","steps":["At a point in a fluid at rest the pressure is the same in every direction.","A quantity with size but no direction is a scalar.","The force on a surface is a vector, but pressure itself is not."],"others":"Pressure is not a vector pointing up or along the force. It is not described as a six-component tensor here.","trap":"Force = pressure × area, and the force is the vector.","answer":"scalar quantity, because it has no direction of its own"},"phy-12-02-m28":{"idea":"Detergent lowers surface tension, which helps water spread and enter small gaps.","steps":["Detergent reduces the surface tension of water.","A lower tension lets the water spread over the fabric and enter small pores.","That lifts the dirt out of the pores."],"others":"Detergent does not mainly change the density, the boiling point or the viscosity.","trap":"Wetting depends on surface tension.","answer":"the water can wet the fabric and enter small pores more easily"},"phy-12-02-n01":{"idea":"Gauge pressure at depth is ρ g h, without the atmosphere.","steps":["ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 10 m/s², h = 20 m.","P = ρ g h = 1000 × 10 × 20 = 2 × 10⁵ Pa.","In kPa: 200.0 kPa."],"trap":"Gauge pressure leaves out P₀.","answer":"200.0 kPa"},"phy-12-02-n02":{"idea":"Pascal's law: pressure is the same on both pistons.","steps":["F₂/A₂ = F₁/A₁.","F₂ = F₁ × A₂/A₁ = 100 × 0.2/0.005.","F₂ = 100 × 40 = 4000 N."],"trap":"Area ratio is 0.2/0.005 = 40.","answer":"4000 N"},"phy-12-02-n03":{"idea":"Buoyant force is the weight of the displaced water.","steps":["Volume displaced = 0.002 m³.","Weight = ρ V g = 1000 × 0.002 × 10.","= 20.0 N."],"trap":"A fully immersed body displaces its own volume.","answer":"20.0 N"},"phy-12-02-n04":{"idea":"Use the equation of continuity with areas proportional to d².","steps":["A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ and A ∝ d².","v₂ = v₁ (d₁/d₂)² = 2 × (4/2)².","v₂ = 2 × 4 = 8.0 m/s."],"trap":"Halving the diameter reduces the area by four.","answer":"8.0 m/s"},"phy-12-02-n05":{"idea":"Torricelli's theorem gives the speed from a hole at depth h.","steps":["v = √(2 g h).","= √(2 × 10 × 5) = √100.","v = 10.0 m/s."],"trap":"Depth is measured from the free surface.","answer":"10.0 m/s"},"phy-12-02-n06":{"idea":"At terminal speed, weight minus upthrust equals the Stokes drag.","steps":["v = 2 r² (ρ − σ) g/(9 η).","r² = (1.0 × 10⁻³)² = 10⁻⁶ m², and ρ − σ = 1500 kg/m³.","v = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 1500 × 10/(9 × 0.1) = 0.03/0.9.","v = 0.033 m/s."],"trap":"Use the difference of densities, not just ρ.","answer":"0.033 m/s"},"phy-12-02-n07":{"idea":"Capillary rise is h = 2T cos θ/(ρ g r).","steps":["T = 0.072 N/m, θ = 0°, so cos θ = 1.","ρ g r = 1000 × 10 × 0.0005 = 5.","h = 2 × 0.072/5 = 0.0288 m = 28.8 mm."],"trap":"Convert mm to metres for the radius.","answer":"28.8 mm"},"phy-12-02-n08":{"idea":"A soap bubble has two surfaces, so ΔP = 4T/R.","steps":["T = 0.03 N/m and R = 0.01 m.","ΔP = 4 × 0.03/0.01.","ΔP = 12.0 Pa."],"trap":"A drop would give half of this.","answer":"12.0 Pa"},"phy-12-02-n09":{"idea":"Work done equals surface tension times the increase in area, counting both film surfaces.","steps":["Area gained = 2 × 4π R² = 8π × (0.02)² = 0.010053 m².","W = T ΔA = 0.03 × 0.010053.","W = 3.016 × 10⁻⁴ J = 301.6 μJ."],"trap":"Two surfaces, so the area is doubled.","answer":"301.6 μJ"},"phy-12-02-n10":{"idea":"The Reynolds number is Re = ρ v D/η.","steps":["ρ = 1000, v = 0.5, D = 0.02, η = 0.001.","ρ v D = 1000 × 0.5 × 0.02 = 10.","Re = 10/0.001 = 10000, so the flow is turbulent."],"trap":"Use the diameter, not the radius.","answer":"10000"}};
